    <h1>Computer Number Systems Explained (Binary and Hex)</h1>
    <p class="lead">Computers store numbers using only two digits, 0 and 1. People usually count with ten digits. This guide explains how the common ways of writing numbers relate to each other, so the numbers you see on screen make sense.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The everyday way: ten digits</h2>
<p>We count with ten digits, 0 to 9. The position of each digit gives its value. In the number 255, the 5 on the right means five ones, the 5 in the middle means fifty, and the 2 on the left means two hundred.</p>
<h2>How computers count: two digits</h2>
<p>Computers use only 0 and 1. Each position is worth double the one to its right. This is called binary. The number 255 is written 11111111 in binary.</p>
<h2>The short form: sixteen symbols</h2>
<p>Writing long rows of 0s and 1s is hard to read, so programmers often use hex, which uses 0 to 9 and then A to F. One hex symbol stands for four binary digits, so 11111111 becomes FF.</p>
<h2>Why this matters to you</h2>
<p>You may see these numbers in colour codes, error messages or software settings. You do not need to calculate them yourself. Use the converter when you see one and want to know what it means.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>Take the number 1010 in computer digits. Counting from the right, the place values are 1, 2, 4 and 8. The places with a 1 add up: 8 + 2 = 10. So 1010 means ten. In the short form it is written A.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Why do computers use only 0 and 1?</summary><p>Computer parts are either on or off. On is written as 1 and off as 0.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why is the short form used?</summary><p>It is shorter and easier to read. One short symbol stands for four computer digits.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I need to know this to use a computer?</summary><p>No. The converter does the sums for you.</p></details>
      </section>
